libfprint-goodix-spi for Debian
===============================

This package ships the out-of-tree Goodix GDIX51C0 SPI driver for libfprint.
It exists because two upstream components have to be modified to make the
sensor work, and neither modification can be expressed as a patch to the
distribution packages:

  * libfprint must be compiled with the GDIX51C0 driver, its private
    lib/goodix/* backend and an SPI udev rule that matches the Goodix ACPI
    modalias (patches/libfprint/1.94.10.patch, applied to v1.94.10);
  * fprintd must learn to persist adaptive "templateStudy" updates and to keep
    a warm device session across Claim/Release and suspend/resume
    (patches/fprintd/1.94.5-goodix-spi.patch, applied to v1.94.5).

Because the patched libfprint is not ABI-compatible with whatever the
distribution ships, the upstream project installs both components as a private
"shadow" pair below /opt. This package does the same. In particular it does
*not* install anything into /usr/lib/<multiarch>, does not run ldconfig and
does not provide a shlibs/symbols entry for the shadow library.


1. What ./install.sh does, and how the package reproduces it
------------------------------------------------------------

  install.sh step                                  package equivalent
  ----------------------------------------------   -------------------------------
  preflight: read config/supported-spi-devices.tsv build: same file is parsed by
  (driver id, ACPI id, install hook)               debian/rules; ids are verified
                                                   against the generated udev rule
  preflight: pkg-config and tool checks            Build-Depends in debian/control
  preflight: probe /sys/bus/spi/... (check_hardware) NOT run - a build host has no
                                                   sensor; equivalent to
                                                   --skip-hardware-check
  fetch libfprint v1.94.10 into the user cache     build: scripts/fetch-libfprint.sh
                                                   into debian/build/sources
  scripts/libfprint/prepare-tree.sh: copy          build: called unchanged
  drivers/, lib/goodix/, integration/ and apply
  the libfprint patch
  meson setup + compile (install profile)          build: equivalent option set,
                                                   see section 4
  install libfprint-2.so.2.0.0 + .so.2 + .so       install: same three files, same
  to /opt/goodix-spi-driver/libfprint              directory, mode 0644 instead of
                                                   0755 (a library needs no execute
                                                   bit; see section 4)
  install generated 70-libfprint-2.rules to         install: same path
  /etc/udev/rules.d, widen ACPI match to a glob
  write 20-goodix-spi-libfprint.conf with          install: composed from the same
  [Service] + LD_LIBRARY_PATH + hook emit output   install hook at build time
  build patched fprintd and install                install: built and installed at
  /opt/goodix-spi-driver/fprintd/fprintd           build time
  write 30-goodix-spi-adaptive.conf                install: same content
  (ExecStart override, warm session, driver list)  (driver list from metadata)
  seed /var/lib/fprint/gdix51c0.psk from           NOT reproduced, see section 6
  GOODIX_TLS_PSK_HEX
  write /var/lib/goodix-spi-driver/install.manifest NOT reproduced: dpkg tracks
                                                   the files instead
  udevadm reload/trigger, systemctl daemon-reload  postinst (and postrm)
  and restart fprintd.service

Installed files:

  /opt/goodix-spi-driver/libfprint/libfprint-2.so.2.0.0   patched libfprint
  /opt/goodix-spi-driver/libfprint/libfprint-2.so.2       -> libfprint-2.so.2.0.0
  /opt/goodix-spi-driver/libfprint/libfprint-2.so         -> libfprint-2.so.2.0.0
  /opt/goodix-spi-driver/fprintd/fprintd                  patched daemon
  /etc/systemd/system/fprintd.service.d/20-goodix-spi-libfprint.conf
  /etc/systemd/system/fprintd.service.d/30-goodix-spi-adaptive.conf
  /etc/udev/rules.d/70-libfprint-2.rules


2. Why everything stays in /opt
-------------------------------

* ABI. The shadow library exports the same SONAME (libfprint-2.so.2) as the
  distribution libfprint but is a different build with a private, unversioned
  driver backend. Registering it in ldconfig or shipping it in a library
  directory would let unrelated processes load it and crash or, worse, load an
  unpatched fprintd against a patched library.
* Upgradability. The pin (libfprint v1.94.10) is not the distribution version.
  Keeping the pair outside /usr means a distribution upgrade of libfprint or
  fprintd cannot half-upgrade the shadow pair; /opt only has to be re-checked
  when the sensor stops working.
* Upstream compatibility. Everything here works the same way whether the user
  ran ./install.sh or installed this package, so the upstream documentation,
  the hook scripts and uninstall.sh keep being meaningful.

Consequences you should know about:

* `dh_makeshlibs` is deliberately disabled (no shlibs file, no ldconfig
  trigger) and `dh_shlibdeps` resolves libfprint-2.so.2 from the package's own
  /opt directory (`--ignore-missing-info`), so no dependency on the
  distribution libfprint package is generated.
* /etc/udev/rules.d/70-libfprint-2.rules has the same file name as the rule
  shipped by libfprint in /usr/lib/udev/rules.d. udev prefers /etc for equal
  names, so the distribution rule is shadowed, not merged. The file installed
  here is generated from a full libfprint build with the default driver set,
  so the other drivers keep their rules. If the distribution libfprint adds a
  new device rule later, it will not take effect until this package is
  rebuilt; `udevadm info` on the new device is the quickest way to notice.
* AppArmor or SELinux confinement of fprintd, if a distribution ever adds it,
  must be extended for /opt/goodix-spi-driver/fprintd/fprintd.


3. Building (target suite: forky)
---------------------------------

This branch targets Debian 14 "forky", the suite that carries the gusb runtime
package rename described at the end of this section. Only binary builds are
supported from a git checkout - there is no upstream tarball and no tagged
release to build a source package from, see README.source:

    dpkg-buildpackage -b -uc -us

Checked in a debian:forky container (debhelper 14.5, meson 1.12.1,
libgusb2a 0.4.9-8, libgpiod3 2.2.5): the build completes, lintian is clean,
the package installs and removes cleanly, and the result contains exactly the
files listed in section 1. Two build dependencies are easy to miss because
install.sh's preflight does not check them:

  * libpixman-1-dev - required by the aes3k imaging helper as soon as the
    default (complete) driver set is built;
  * systemd-dev - provides udev.pc on trixie and later; libudev-dev alone only
    ships libudev.pc, and libfprint's meson asks for udev.

Both are in Build-Depends.

Note that the build needs network access: the pinned upstream libfprint and
fprintd trees are fetched with the project's own fetch scripts into
debian/build/sources (removed by `debian/rules clean`). This is a deliberate
deviation from "no network during build" - packaging a moving target that
pins two other projects cannot be done from a single source tarball. The
fetched revisions are fixed (`v1.94.10`, `v1.94.5`) and the checkout is
verified per ref, so the build is reproducible as long as those refs exist.

libgpiod >= 2.0 is required by the driver. Releases that still ship libgpiod
1.x (for example Debian bookworm and Ubuntu 24.04) cannot satisfy the
Build-Depends; build in a newer chroot/container instead of weakening the
version constraint.

Rebuild on the suite you are going to install on. dpkg-shlibdeps derives
Depends from the shlibs/symbols files of the build host, so the dependency
names are those of the *build* suite, not of the target. forky renamed the
gusb runtime package libgusb2 -> libgusb2a while its SONAME stayed
libgusb.so.2, so a package built on trixie is not installable on forky
although the ABI is identical:

    libfprint-goodix-spi : Depends: libgusb2 (>= 0.3.3) but it is not installable

Building this branch on forky produces libgusb2a in Depends and installs
cleanly; that single name is the only difference between the two builds. The
+forky1 suffix in debian/changelog keeps them distinguishable - bump that
suffix (dch --local) rather than reusing it if you rebuild elsewhere.

No debian/watch file is provided: upstream has no tagged releases yet, so the
package version follows the "0.0~git<date>.<commit>" snapshot scheme.


4. Build options, and why they differ from install.sh
-----------------------------------------------------

install.sh builds with the Meson defaults plus -Dgoodix_spi_developer=false.
debian/rules additionally passes:

    -Dudev_rules=enabled -Dudev_hwdb=disabled -Ddoc=false
    -Dintrospection=false -Dinstalled-tests=false -Dgtk-examples=false

None of these change the two shipped artifacts (libfprint-2.so.2.0.0 and
70-libfprint-2.rules). They only avoid building documentation, GObject
introspection data and example programs, which would otherwise have to be
thrown away before packaging and would pull in extra build dependencies.
udev_rules stays enabled because the generated rule is a shipped artifact.
Every option above is already used by the project's own CI profile
(scripts/libfprint/build.sh --profile ci), so the option names are known to be
valid for the pinned revision.
The driver set is left at the upstream default so that the generated udev rule
covers every driver of the distribution libfprint, whose rule it shadows.

The build also strips a build-tree RPATH from the shadow fprintd binary if
Meson left one (install.sh copies the binary as built, which can leave the
build path of the packager inside the ELF file). At run time the library is
found through LD_LIBRARY_PATH from the drop-in, as intended.

One cosmetic difference: install.sh installs libfprint-2.so.2.0.0 with mode
0755. The package installs it 0644, because a shared object does not need the
execute bit and lintian treats an executable .so as an error. The symlinks and
the daemon binary (0755) are unchanged.


5. After installation
---------------------

The package does not touch /etc/fprintd.conf and does not enroll anything.
Test with an existing distribution tool:

    fprintd-list "$USER"
    fprintd-enroll -f right-index-finger
    fprintd-verify

GPIO lines: the defaults match the machine the driver was developed on. If
your laptop wires the sensor differently, do not edit the packaged drop-in
directly - create /etc/systemd/system/fprintd.service.d/99-local-gpio.conf
with, for example:

    [Service]
    Environment=GDIX51C0_GPIOCHIP=/dev/gpiochip1
    Environment=GDIX51C0_IRQ_LINE=42
    Environment=GDIX51C0_RESET_LINE=41

and run `systemctl daemon-reload && systemctl restart fprintd`. The hook
documented in config/install.d/gdix51c0 also accepts GDIX51C0_PSK_STATE_FILE
and GDIX51C0_CHICAGO_CALIBRATION_FILE. Developer-only capture and fault
injection variables are compiled out of the release build shipped here.

Disabling the patched daemon (the equivalent of `./install.sh
--without-fprintd`): the distribution fprintd then runs against the shadow
library, which keeps the driver working but loses adaptive-template
persistence and warm sessions.

    rm /etc/systemd/system/fprintd.service.d/30-goodix-spi-adaptive.conf
    systemctl daemon-reload && systemctl restart fprintd

The file is a conffile, so a later package upgrade may offer it again.


5.1 "No devices available" while the daemon is running
------------------------------------------------------

    fprintd-enroll -f right-index-finger
    Impossible to enroll: GDBus.Error:net.reactivated.Fprint.Error.NoSuchDevice:
    No devices available

while fprintd.service is active and executing the shadow binary. This message
means libfprint enumerated *zero* devices; it is not a failure to open one
(that would read "Device unavailable"). libfprint has no hotplug monitor: it
enumerates once, at startup, by walking the udev "spidev" subsystem and
matching each device's sysfs path against the driver's ACPI id. So the whole
chain must exist at start time:

    /sys/bus/spi/devices/spi-GDIX51C0:00  ->  /sys/class/spidev/spidevX.Y

Check in this order:

    pid=$(pgrep -x fprintd)
    grep -i libfprint /proc/$pid/maps | awk '{print $6}' | sort -u
    #   must be /opt/goodix-spi-driver/libfprint/libfprint-2.so.2.0.0.
    #   The distribution library has the same SONAME, so a missing drop-in
    #   degrades silently to an unpatched libfprint that has no gdix51c0.

    systemctl show fprintd -p Environment
    #   must contain LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/opt/goodix-spi-driver/libfprint

    ls /sys/bus/spi/devices/ /sys/class/spidev/

If the last command shows nothing while a controller exists
(/sys/class/spi_master/spi0), the firmware is not exposing the sensor, and
reloading udev rules cannot help: the kernel creates SPI slaves only for ACPI
devices that report themselves present, and it skips all others before it
reads their _CRS - silently, without a kernel message.

One machine in this state (Huawei KLVF-XX) has the ACPI device but:

    cat /sys/bus/acpi/devices/GDIX51C0:00/status   ->  0

because its _STA is

    Method (_STA) { If (HFTE) { Return (0x0F) } Return (Zero) }

with HFTE cleared. That device's _CRS is otherwise complete and correct (SPI,
ResourceSource \_SB.PC00.SPI0 matching the live controller, 10 MHz, one GpioIo
for reset and one GpioInt for the interrupt), so the _STA answer is the only
thing missing. Note that _INI does not run in that state either, and _INI is
what enables the sensor.

Remedy is firmware side and outside this package: enable the device in firmware
(or in a Windows installation that provisions it), or override the ACPI table
so that _STA returns 0x0F, shipped through the early initrd
(CONFIG_ACPI_TABLE_UPGRADE). An override has to be regenerated after every BIOS
update.

Once the spidev node exists, restart fprintd - it will not be picked up
otherwise.


6. Sensor key, calibration and enrolled prints
----------------------------------------------

The driver authorizes one random first-run TLS provisioning and stores the key
as /var/lib/fprint/gdix51c0.psk (root, mode 0600); calibration is stored next
to it. These files are created by the driver at run time, not by this package,
and they are deliberately *not* removed on purge: the sensor keeps its copy of
the key, so deleting the host side makes the pair unrecoverable without a
sensor reset, and is not something a package manager should do silently. The
same reasoning applies to enrolled fingerprints in /var/lib/fprint.

Cleanup, if that is really what you want, after `apt purge`:

    rm -f /var/lib/fprint/gdix51c0.psk \
          /var/lib/fprint/gdix51c0-calibration.bin \
          /var/lib/fprint/gdix51c0-image-base.bin \
          /var/lib/fprint/gdix51c0-dark-frame.bin

To reuse a key that already exists (for example one provisioned by a previous
./install.sh run or imported from a Windows installation on a dual-boot
machine), write it *before* fprintd starts for the first time:

    install -d -m 0700 /var/lib/fprint
    printf '%s\n' "$GOODIX_TLS_PSK_HEX" > /var/lib/fprint/gdix51c0.psk
    chmod 0600 /var/lib/fprint/gdix51c0.psk
    systemctl restart fprintd

Upgrading from an ./install.sh installation: run ./uninstall.sh first. The
installer keeps its own manifest in /var/lib/goodix-spi-driver and would
otherwise delete files that dpkg believes it owns. The postinst warns when
that manifest is still present.


7. Security model (quoted from upstream, and worth repeating)
-------------------------------------------------------------

The GDIX51C0 streams images to the host; every match runs on the CPU. It is
not match-on-chip, there is no liveness detection, and a compromised host
defeats it. Treat fingerprint unlock as a convenience factor, not as the only
line of defence.

 -- libfprint-goodix-spi packagers <packagers@example.invalid>
